So here are the details: Since the CN units are single-probe units,
each chip effect estimate is theta = y, where y is the probe
intensity.  That is just a matter of "copy-and-paste" (read and write)
from the probe intensity CEL file to the chip effect CEL file.  Can't
be faster than that.  For the SNP units, we have three or four pairs
of (yA,yB).  If you do combineAlleles=TRUE, we first calculate y=yA+yB
per pair.  Then we "fit" the model.  With AvgCnPlm that estimator
becomes median(y) over the three or four allele sums.  This is really
fast too.  What makes it slow, is that we are leaning on the
multi-chip array framework developed for the RMA-style log-additive
models fitting chip effects and probe affinities.  In this setup with
AvgCnPlm all probe affinities are by definition 1 (one).  This
framework process the data in chunks of units and reads the probe
intensities *across all arrays* in to a list structure.  There is a
substantial overhead of wrapping everything up in a list structure
containing a chunk of units.  Then when we fit the model we traverse
this list unit by unit.  The output is another nested list structure
containing parameter estimates (theta, phi, stdTheta, phiTheta, ...)
*across* all arrays.  The final step is to store the estimates, which
means we have to traverse the parameter list unit by unit, identify
and extract the elements that belongs to array 1 and save the to the
corresponding chip effect CEL file, then we do the same for array 2,
and so on.  So, we spend most of the time traversing these list
structure.

The reason for working with list structures like this, is that it is
completely generic to whatever CDF and pretty much whatever PLM we
use.  If all units/probesets had the same number of probes, then we
could work with the data/parameters in a rectangular matrix instead,
avoiding all the overhead of the lists.  However, this is not the case
most chip types/CDFs.  One possible to achieve this though is to group
the units into blocks of equal number of probes per units/unit group.
This is likely to speed up things substantially (i.e. tons).
However, one need to consider several use cases/chip types/CDFs/PLMs
when designing such a thing.  Things like combineAlleles,
mergeStrands, mergeGroups, and other "PLM restructers" complicates it
a bit.  Single-array PLMs such as the AvgPlm:s make it easier, so one
probably want to start thinking about those.

So, if someone is really keen on getting significantly improved probe
summarization, please have a look at the above and contributions with
suggestions, design and code is appreciated.

