Hi again.

For the record, I can confirm that Windows (the Windows API) has a
maximum-length limitation on pathnames (path + filename) of 260
symbols including the drive letter, colon and slash, e.g
C:/Users/hb/foo/bar.txt has nchar("C:/Users/hb/foo/bar.txt") == 23
symbols.  For details, see Section 'Maximum Path Length' in MSDN
document 'Naming a File or Directory':

  http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/aa365247.aspx

The workaround is to mount a new device letter to a path and use that
as a new "root" path.  See previous message (below) on how to do this.

Comment/for the record: The above MSDN document talks about a Unicode
format with prefix "\\?\" (or "//?/"), which can handle much longer
pathnames (32,768 symbols).  It seems that these are (automatically?)
supported by R, e.g. list.files(path="//?/C:/").  However, if you
create a too long pathname, for instance via subst and then "go back"
to the original path, R complaints about too long pathnames.  I leave
it at this for now, because the above subst workaround should do.

Cheers

/Henrik

On Sat, Dec 27, 2008 at 1:06 PM, Henrik Bengtsson <h...@stat.berkeley.edu> wrote:
> Hi,
>
> I stumbled on a solution/workaround for Windows.  With the command
> 'subst' you can assign a device letter to any pathname.  In your case,
> you can do something like:
>
> # Mount drive letter X: to long path
> rootPath <- "C:/Documents and Settings/Elizabeth/My
> Documents/Research/ExonArrays/aroma.affymetrix.work/MouseHuman/Filter_80PctJt <http://aroma.affymetrix.work/MouseHuman/Filter_80PctJt>";
> cmd <- sprintf('subst X: "%s"', rootPath);
> system(cmd);
>
> Now, have start R with X:/ as the working directory, instead of
> "C:/Documents...".  Similarly, you can have Windows Shortcuts to point
> to such mounted devices.
>
> # Unmount drive letter X:
> system("subst X: /D");
>
> /Henrik
>
